+### Dungeons and Planechase in the view
+
+`DungeonView` writes `PlayerDto.dungeonState` (`name`, `room`, the dungeon card's `cardId`, and `rooms`: each room's `RoomName`, its `NextRoomName` list and its description, read from the dungeon's triggers as `VentureEffect.chooseNextRoom` reads them) and `completedDungeons` (names). The fix directive's `dungeon`, `completedDungeon` and `initiative` ops make the dungeon token the way `VentureEffect.getDungeonCard` does; they run with triggers suppressed like every first-pass op, so taking the initiative there ventures nowhere.
+
+A start request's `players[].planes` turns Planechase on (`Planechase.planarDecks`): when every player brought a plane each keeps their own deck, otherwise the pool is dealt round-robin, planes before phenomena, so every planar deck holds a plane (`Player.initPlane` loops forever on a phenomena-only deck and `Player.planeswalk` reads past an empty one). `GameViewDto.planar` carries the controller, the active plane ids, the die cost (`getPlanarDiceSpecialActionThisTurn`), each planar deck's size and the last roll. Forge tells every seat a roll with `notifyOfValue`; the controller records it once (`Planechase.noteRoll`) instead of asking for an acknowledgement. The `planeswalk` and `chaos` ops run in the free-play pass, after triggers are back on, so the plane's triggers fire. Unlike the free plays they leave priority where it was: handing it to the op's player, who might then leave the game, kept `PhaseHandler`'s `pFirstPriority` on a lost player and the step never ended. A `planeswalk` op walks the plane's owner to it, the way a real planeswalk comes from the walker's own deck (Forge keeps the planes in play in that player's `currentPlanes`, the same list as `Game.activePlanes`), then gives the planes to the active player as `PhaseHandler` does at each turn (rule 901.6). A Planechase game takes no restore points: `GameSnapshot` copies neither `Game.activePlanes` nor `Player.currentPlanes`.
+
+### Effects, delayed triggers and turn counts in the view
+
+Forge's Effect cards (`isImmutable`) stay out of the command zone list; `EffectsView.effects` sends them as `PlayerDto.effects` instead, but only those Forge itself would show: `getRenderForUI()` (so unearth's and the Prepared Spell's helper effects stay hidden), an effect source (the player's built-in Monarch, Initiative, Ring, Commander, Keyword and other effects have none) and non-empty ability text. `untilEndOfTurn` is set for an `Effect` or `Fog` source with no `Duration$` (both end at cleanup); otherwise the source's raw `Duration$` goes out as `duration`. `GameViewDto.delayed` lists `TriggerHandler`'s delayed triggers (read by reflection, both fields named in `reflect-config.json`), immediate (reflexive) ones excepted, plus the player-defined ones still waiting for that player's next turn (`turnOf`); `when` is worded from a `Phase` trigger's `Phase$`, and `cardIds` are the trigger's remembered cards. Per viewer, a host or remembered card the viewer may not see (`shownTo`: a hand, a library) is left out of `hostCardId`/`cardIds`, and then `text` is sent empty, since Forge's text names it. Forge writes card names with their engine id (`Card.toString`, "Hill Giant (2)"); the ids of the cards involved are taken out of names and texts. Triggers still parked in a cleanup `addUntil` (`NextTurn$`, `UpcomingTurn$`) are not in the handler yet and are not sent. `spellsCastThisTurn`, `lifeGainedThisTurn`, `lifeLostThisTurn` and `stormCount` (`MagicStack.getSpellsCastThisTurn().size()`: the copies a storm spell cast now would get) are sent only when above zero, and every new field is absent when empty, so an older reader sees the same view.
+
+### Teams and Two-Headed Giant (`teamRules`)
+
+`StartGameRequest` takes an optional game-level `teamRules` (`none`, the default; `teams`; `sharedLife`), `poisonToLose`, `commanderDamageToLose`, and per player `team`, `startingLife`, `startingHand`; the self-hosted node fills them from the room controller's `gameConfig` roomRelay message. `configureTeamRules` refuses an unknown rule, a missing or negative team, fewer than two teams, a `poisonToLose` or `commanderDamageToLose` below 1, and for `sharedLife` uneven teams, partners apart in seat order (the order wraps round, so `1,2,2,1` is two adjacent pairs) and partners given different `startingLife`. `sharedLife` sets Forge's `GameRules.setSharedTeamLife` (the 2HG rules live in Forge, `engine/forge.patch`: life and poison mirrored to teammates, a team loses together, teammates block for each other, the team's first seat starts and the starting team skips its first draws, a commander's damage summed over the team) and the defaults by team size `heads`: poison `15 + 5 * (heads - 2)`, commander damage `21 * heads`, starting life `40 * heads` in Commander and `30 + 15 * (heads - 2)` otherwise. Life and poison are mirrored, not pooled: every teammate's `life` is the team total, so readers need no team arithmetic. The first-player roll offers only each team's first seat (`GameAction.teamLead`). `GameViewDto.teamRules` (`rule`, `poisonToLose`, `commanderDamageToLose` in Commander) is sent only when a team has two players; a teammate's hand is shown to the viewer in a `sharedLife` game (`sharesHandWith`, CR 810.5). The Rust engine backend has no team rules: the node logs a warning and starts such a game without its `gameConfig`. A `teams` game without `sharedLife` keeps Forge's ordinary win: when one player wins, everyone else loses, teammates included. `TeamGameTest` pins each rule with real cards.
+
+Per player, three more settings (Juggernaut, `configurePlayerRules`): `perks`, a list of card-script lines (`Name:`, `Oracle:`, `SVar:`, `T:`, `S:`, `R:`; anything else refuses the start) that `Perks` turns into one immutable effect card in that player's command zone after `match.createGame()`, so its triggers, statics and replacements are rules of the game (an immutable card without an effect source is in neither the command zone nor `effects` of the view); `commanderDamageToLose`, that player's own limit (Forge `RegisteredPlayer.setCommanderDamageToLose`, read by `Player.getCommanderDamageToLose`, which falls back to the game's); and `goesFirst`, at most one player, who then starts (Forge `GameRules.setStartingPlayer`, ahead of the roll). Forge's `ReplaceBeginTurn` takes `ExtraTurn$ False` (only a turn that is not an extra one), which the Juggernaut's "after every challenger" order uses. `PlayerRulesTest` pins them.
+
+### A controlled player's prompts go to the controlling seat (Mindslaver)
+
+`Player.addController` gives a controlled player a controller made by the controlling player's lobby player, so an interactive seat that takes control of a Forge AI seat (Mindslaver) answers through `ManaBrewInteractiveController` under the AI's seat index. `publishAgentPrompt` therefore names the seat that decides, not the seat the question is about: `controllingSeat` follows `getControllingPlayer` up the chain (A controls B who controls C: C's questions go to A, since A makes every decision B would make) and stops at a controller whose original lobby player is not a `ManaBrewInteractiveLobbyPlayer` (a Forge AI cannot answer a prompt, so the question stays with the last interactive seat) or at a player it has already visited (two players controlling each other). `promptedPlayerIndex` follows `decidingPlayerId`: the browser bridge exchanges with the controlling seat, and it is the controlling seat's concede, not the controlled player's, that turns the open prompt into a pass. `MindslaverControlTest` pins one-level, nested, AI-topped and looping control.
+
+### A defender choice names its attacker
+
+`chooseSingleEntityForEffect` over a mixed list (players and permanents) is asked as a `chooseFromSelection` of Forge's `toString` labels. Its title goes out as the presentation's `description`, and when Forge passes an `Attacker` param (`SpellAbilityEffect.addToCombat`, a creature put onto the battlefield attacking, CR 506.3b: Hero of Bladehold's tokens) that creature is the presentation's one `CARD` target, so a client can tell which of two identical tokens a question is for. `AttackDefenderChoiceTest` resolves Hero's real token ability and checks each question names the token that then attacks the chosen defender.
+
+### The attackers prompt carries Forge's taxes, refusals and limits
+
+`publishAttackersPrompt` fills, from Forge's own combat rules rather than card text: each attacker's `costs`, one per legal defender with a cost (`CombatUtil.getAttackCost`'s total as `Cost.toSimpleString`, plus the statics whose `getAttackCost` is not null as `sourceIds`/`sourceNames`); `unavailable`, one entry per creature that can't attack at all (no `defenderId`) or can't attack one defender, with the first reason in `CombatUtil.canAttack`'s own order (`tapped`, `phasedOut`, `summoningSick`, `goaded`, `keyword`, `detained`, `defender`, `static` with its source, else `other`); and `limits`, one per `AttackRestrict` static (`max`, and `defenderId` when it has `ValidDefender`). A rule hosted on an immutable effect card is named after `getEffectSource`, so War Tax's effect reads War Tax. `unavailable` and `limits` are always serialized, even empty, so a client can tell this engine from one that sends neither.
