+
+`GameViewDto.events` is a ring of the last 512 zone changes and damage events, from `EngineEventLog`, which the session subscribes to the game in `attach`. The same ring goes to every seat and each entry has a rising `seq`, so a client that misses a snapshot loses nothing and a gap shows it fell behind. Each entry also carries an `id`: the relay's state patches key an array by `id`, and without one every new entry resends the whole ring. Forge fires no draw or discard event, so one entry carries `how`, set from the event order: `sacrificed` from the event just before the move, `destroyed` from `CardView.wasDestroyed` (`GameAction` sets it on a destroy and on a state-based death but fires `GameEventCardDestroyed` only on the first, and sets it on a sacrifice too, so `sacrificed` is checked first), `played`, `discard` and `surveil` from the event just after it, and `draw` from `Card.getDrawnThisTurn`, read at the next move, phase or snapshot because Forge sets it after the move (at the next move, or a card drawn and then discarded in one resolution, as Faithless Looting does, moves before its draw is read and loses it); `mill` likewise from `Card.wasMilled`, read at the next move or snapshot because `Player.mill` sets it after `moveTo`; and, the same way, `foretell`, `plot` and `suspend` for a card still in exile with `isForetold`, `isPlotted` or `hasSuspend` (special actions resolve off the stack, so no ability names them). `GameEventCardChangeZone` carries no cause, so `cause` is read from the game at the move: a move made while `ReplacementHandler.isReplacing()` takes the next `EFFECT_REPLACED` log message (Dredge's reads `<card> - Dredge N`); a move made while `game.costPaymentStack` holds a cost part takes `Cost:<name>`, the name being `Delve` for a card exiled while the mana part of a delve card is paid, else the paying ability's keyword (`Cycling`, `TypeCycling`), else its alternative cost (`Escape`, `Emerge`), else the cost part's class without `Cost` (`Sacrifice`, `Discard`, `Exile`), with the ability's host as `causeName`; and otherwise a move made while the stack resolves takes the resolving ability's `ApiType` name (`Explore`, `Mill`, `Surveil`), with its host as `causeName`/`causeCardId`. Moves off the stack never get a cause. Moves onto the stack and moves before `GameStage.Play` are skipped. An entry names its card only when a public zone is involved and the card isn't face down; foretell turns its card face down after the move, so an exile entry whose card is face down in exile when the move settles loses its name too; a draw stays a count. Moves made by a restore are ignored. Damage entries (`kind: "damage"`, `to: "Damage"`, so a client that compares `to` with zone names skips them; no `cardId`) take the same `seq` as moves, carry `sourceCardId`, `targetCardId` or `targetPlayerId`, `amount` and `combat`, and `ownerId` is the source's owner. One whose source has no owner in the game, or whose target has no id in the view (a token that died before any snapshot saw it), is not sent, so `seq` can skip a number. A player's comes whole from `GameEventPlayerDamaged`. A card's is read from the `Pair` that `CardDamageHistory.registerDamage` adds to the target's `getDamageReceivedThisTurn()` (amount, combat flag, and the source by `Game.getDamageLKI`), at the next event or snapshot, since Forge fires `GameEventCardDamaged` before registering it and gives it no combat flag; a non-creature planeswalker or battle fires no damage event at all, so a drop in its loyalty or defense counters is waited on the same way and kept only when the received list grew (a loyalty cost does not). Prevented damage is never dealt, so it sends the amount left, or nothing. The ring is 512 so that one board's wipe (a damage entry and a death per creature) does not push out moves a client has not read. `EngineEventLog` is named in `native/extra-config/reflect-config.json` like `GameLogFormatter` in the frozen config: Guava finds `@Subscribe` by reflection, so a subscriber missing from that file receives nothing in the native image while working on a JVM.
 
